{"id": "c265b2de-bfc7-4657-9308-ac73c41de690", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is really a conceptual AWS networking question I can reason through directly rather than needing to look anything up. VPC endpoints for S3 are region-specific, so a Gateway or Interface endpoint only works for S3 buckets in the same region as the VPC—cross-region S3 traffic would still need to route out through the NAT Gateway regardless of endpoint type.

The real fix for genuinely cross-region reads would be relocating the data or bucket, using Cross-Region Replication so reads happen locally through an endpoint, or accepting that NAT costs persist since endpoints only help when traffic stays in-region. I want to verify this claim about S3 VPC endpoints being strictly regional before stating it as fact.

