{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS PrivateLink now supports cross-region connectivity for AWS Services - AWS","context":"AWS PrivateLink, a service that allows access to AWS services through private endpoints within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), now supports cross-region connectivity. Previously, Interface VPC endpoints only enabled access to services within the same region. With this update, customers can now connect to select AWS services, such as Amazon S3, Route53, and Elastic Container Registry (ECR), in other regions through Interface endpoints. This eliminates the need for setting up cross-region peering or exposing data over the public internet. The feature simplifies and enhances security for building globally distributed private networks that adhere to data residency requirements. For pricing information and a list of supported services and regions, refer to the AWS PrivateLink pricing page and documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/aws-privatelink-cross-region-connectivity-aws-services/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS PrivateLink now supports cross-region connectivity for AWS Services - AWS","context":"AWS PrivateLink, a service that allows access to AWS services through private endpoints within a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC), now supports cross-region connectivity. Previously, Interface VPC endpoints only enabled access to services within the same region. With this update, customers can now connect to select AWS services, such as Amazon S3, Route53, and Elastic Container Registry (ECR), in other regions through Interface endpoints. This eliminates the need for setting up cross-region peering or exposing data over the public internet. The feature simplifies and enhances security for building globally distributed private networks that adhere to data residency requirements. For pricing information and a list of supported services and regions, refer to the AWS PrivateLink pricing page and documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/aws-privatelink-cross-region-connectivity-aws-services/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Redshift now supports cross-Region queries for your data lake - AWS","context":"Amazon Redshift, a data warehousing service by AWS, has introduced two new capabilities: cross-Region queries for Amazon S3 data lake tables and enhanced VPC routing. These features enable enterprises and security-sensitive customers to query S3 data in different AWS Regions directly, without the need for replication or copying. Cross-Region queries simplify global analytics, consolidate reporting, and adhere to data residency requirements. Enhanced VPC routing ensures that data lake query traffic between Amazon S3 and Amazon Redshift remains within the VPC, preventing data transfer over public networks. Both capabilities are available in all regions where RG provisioned and Serverless clusters are supported. Cross-Region queries incur standard data transfer charges, and regulated industries such as financial services, healthcare, and government can benefit from this feature due to its compliance-friendly nature","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/redshift-cross-Region-queries-for-data-lake/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access","skill_description":"Configures VPC endpoints (interface and gateway) for private AWS service access using AWS PrivateLink. Use when setting up secure private connectivity to S3, DynamoDB, and other AWS services without internet gateway, NAT device, or public IP addresses. Covers endpoint creation, security groups, route tables, and DNS configuration.\n\nServices: ec2, vpc, s3, dynamodb\nTasks: configure\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: networking","skill_name":"configuring-vpc-endpoints-for-private-aws-service-access"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"transitgateway","skill_description":"Configures AWS Transit Gateway: creating a hub and attaching VPCs, segmenting traffic with route tables, centralizing egress and forcing east-west traffic between VPCs through a central firewall via transit gateway route tables and appliance-mode attachments, connecting on-premises networks over the transit gateway side of a Site-to-Site VPN or Direct Connect attachment (including ECMP to aggregate bandwidth across multiple VPN tunnels), peering transit gateways across Regions, migrating from a VPC peering mesh, and routing IP multicast. Applicable when the transit gateway is the resource being configured: connecting many VPCs, isolating environments, routing egress or east-west traffic through the hub for inspection, reaching on-premises over the hub, linking Regions, or moving off a peering mesh. Not applicable for single-VPC routing, VPC peering between two VPCs (vpcpeering skill), Direct Connect gateway or virtual interface setup (directconnect skill), or Route 53 DNS work.\n\n\nServices: transitgateway, vpc, directconnect, sitetositevpn, gatewayloadbalancer\nTasks: configure, secure, deploy, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, network-admin, security-engineer, sre\nWorkload: networking, security, hybrid, multi-region","skill_name":"transitgateway"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verifying whether S3 VPC endpoints support cross-region bucket access."}

